###### Abstract

Electric currents j flow along the open magnetic field lines from the polar caps of neutron stars. Activity of a polar cap depends on the ratio \\alpha=j/c\\rho_{\\rm GJ}, where \\rho_{\\rm GJ} is the corotation charge density. The customary assumption \\alpha\\approx 1 is not supported by recent simulations of pulsar magnetospheres and we study polar caps with arbitrary \\alpha. We argue that no significant activity is generated on field lines with 0<\\alpha<1. Charges are extracted from the star and flow along such field lines with low energies. By contrast, if \\alpha>1 or \\alpha<0, a high voltage is generated, leading to unsteady e^{\\pm} discharge on a scale-height smaller than the size of the polar cap. The discharge can power observed pulsars. Voltage fluctuations in the discharge imply unsteady twisting of the open flux tube and generation of Alfven waves. These waves are ducted along the tube and converted to electromagnetic waves, providing a new mechanism for pulsar radiation. plasmas -- stars: magnetic fields, neutron

## 1 Introduction

Corotation of a plasma magnetosphere is impossible beyond the light cylinder of a star, and magnetic field lines that extend beyond this cylinder are twisted, \\nabla\\times\\,{\\bf B}\\neq 0. Thus, currents {\\bf j}_{B}=(c/4\\pi)\\nabla\\times\\,{\\bf B} are induced in the open magnetic flux tubes that connect the star (its "polar caps") to the light cylinder (Sturrock, 1971). These currents are approximately force-free and flow along the magnetic field \\,{\\bf B}. A basic question of pulsar theory is what voltage develops along the open tube to maintain these currents. It determines the dissipated power and e^{\\pm} creation that feeds the observed activity of pulsars.

The customary pulsar model assumes that the electric current j_{B} extracted from the polar cap nearly matches c\\rho_{\\rm GJ}, where \\rho_{\\rm GJ}=-{\\bf\\Omega}\\cdot\\,{\\bf B}/2\\pi c is the corotation charge density(Goldreich & Julian 1969). The deviation of current from c\\rho_{\\rm GJ} was calculated as an eigen value of an electrostatic problem and found to be small (Arons & Scharlemann 1979). This is in conflict with recent global models of pulsar magnetospheres, which report |j_{B}-c\\rho_{\\rm GJ}|\\sim j_{B} (e.g. Contopoulos et al. 1999; Spitkovsky 2006; Timokhin 2006; see Arons 2008 for a review). A significant mismatch between j_{B} and c\\rho_{\\rm GJ} can be expected on general grounds (Kennel et al. 1979). Currents j_{B} are determined by the magnetic-field twisting near the light cylinder, while \\rho_{\\rm GJ} is a local quantity at the polar cap that is practically independent of j_{B}.

The open tube is surrounded by the grounded closed magnetosphere1 and may be thought of as a waveguide, filled with magnetized (1D) plasma. Compared to usual plasma-filled waveguides, it has two special features: (1) Current j_{B} is imposed on the tube. The twisted tube extends into the star, which is a good conductor and maintains j_{B}. {\\rm sign}(j_{B})=\\pm 1 corresponds to \\pm charge flowing outward along the magnetic field lines. (2) Vacuum has effective charge density \\rho_{0}=-\\rho_{\\rm GJ} as Gauss law in the rotating frame reads \\nabla\\cdot\\,{\\bf E}=4\\pi(\\rho-\\rho_{\\rm GJ}). The key dimensionless parameter (which can vary along and across the tube) is

Footnote 1: The closed magnetosphere with j_{B}=0 is expected to have \\rho=\\rho_{\\rm GJ} and E_{\\parallel}\\approx 0 (e.g. Arons 1979).

\\alpha=\\frac{j_{B}}{c\\rho_{\\rm GJ}}. 

In this Letter, we discuss basic properties of the polar-cap accelerator with arbitrary \\alpha. First, we discuss what happens without e^{\\pm} creation: SS 2 studies how the current is extracted from the polar cap of a radius r_{\\rm pc} and flows at small heights z\\ll r_{\\rm pc} (this region is called "slab zone" below), and SS 3 discusses how the flow extends to the region z>r_{\\rm pc} ("thin-tube zone"). We argue that the accelerator is inefficient if 0<\\alpha<1.

The value of \\alpha depends largely on the angle \\chi between \\bf\\Omega and \\,{\\bf B} at the polar cap. For aligned dipole rotators (\\chi=0), magnetospheric models predict \\alpha<1 everywhere on the polar cap and \\alpha<0 near its edge (see Fig. 4 and 5 in Timokhin 2006). For orthogonal rotator (\\chi\\approx\\pi/2), |\\alpha|\\gg 1 throughout most of the polar cap. Generally, the polar cap has three regions where \\alpha>1, 0<\\alpha<1, and \\alpha<0. We propose that pulsar activity originates in the polar-cap regions where \\alpha^{-1}<1 (i.e. \\alpha>1 or \\alpha<0) as a high voltage is generated in these regions.

We emphasize that the voltage is generated because \\nabla\\times\\,{\\bf B}\\neq 0, not because \\rho_{\\rm GJ}\\neq 0. The accelerator works as well if \\rho_{\\rm GJ}=0 (i.e. if \\bf\\Omega\\perp\\,{\\bf B} at the polar cap), which corresponds to \\alpha\\rightarrow\\pm\\infty. The accelerator height h\\mathrel{\\hbox to 0.0pt{\\raise 2.1973pt\\hbox{$<$}}{\\lower 2.1973pt\\hbox{$ \\sim$}}}r_{\\rm pc} is regulated by unsteady e^{\\pm} discharges (SS 4). SS 5 describes a mechanism for radio emission from unsteady discharges.

Acceleration of a charge-separated flow at z\\ll r_{\\rm pc}

Suppose the current j_{B} is carried by charges e extracted from the polar cap; e<0 if j_{B}<0. We assume that this charge-separated flow is freely supplied by the star with initial velocity v\\ll c. Let us look for a steady state in the rotating frame of the star. The electrostatic field \\,{\\bf E} is vertical near the surface (except at the edge of the polar cap). Since the displacement current c^{-1}\\partial\\,{\\bf E}/\\partial t=0, the conduction current \\,{\\bf j} satisfies \\,{\\bf j}=\\,{\\bf j}_{B}\\equiv(c/4\\pi)\\,\\nabla\\times\\,{\\bf B}. All particles are in the ground Landau state and flow along the magnetic field lines. Their motion is governed by the electric-field component E_{\\parallel} (parallel to \\,{\\bf B}),

\\frac{dp}{dt}=\\frac{eE_{\\parallel}}{mc},  where m is the charge mass and p is its momentum in units of mc. Gravity is negligible here (in contrast to a similar problem for closed field lines, Beloborodov & Thompson 2007).

Let \\psi be the angle between \\,{\\bf B} and the vertical z-axis. Using \\nabla\\cdot\\,{\\bf E}\\approx dE_{z}/dz for the electrostatic field near the surface (z\\ll r_{\\rm pc}), one finds from the Gauss law, dE_{\\parallel}/dz\\approx 4\\pi\\cos\\psi(\\rho-\\rho_{\\rm GJ}). The flow has charge density \\rho=j/v where v=cp(1+p^{2})^{-1/2}. In a steady state, one can rewrite Gauss equation using the convective derivative d/dt\\equiv\\cos\\psi\\,v\\,d/dz,

