## 3 Extension to the thin-tube zone z>r_{\\rm pc} and time dependence

We still discuss here flows with no e^{\\pm} creation. The effects of e^{\\pm} are addressed in SS 4. Consider a solution from SS 2 with \\alpha^{-1}>1 and period z_{0}\\ll r_{\\rm pc} (Fig. 1). The flow returns to its initial conditions at z=0 after passing distance z_{0}, i.e., effectively, the location of the polar-cap surface is shifted to z_{n}=nz_{0} (n=1,2,...). Therefore, the flow remains in the slab regime and its periodic acceleration/deceleration continues at z>r_{\\rm pc} with a small oscillating potential drop2e\\Phi/m_{e}c^{2}=(1+p_{\\rm max}^{2})^{1/2}-1=2\\alpha^{2}(1-\\alpha^{2})^{-1}. The parameter \\alpha and \\Phi gradually change on scale \\sim radius r\\gg z_{0}. Thus, a modest voltage sustains the charge flow along the bundle of field lines with 0<\\alpha<1. Energy is released with rate \\sim I\\Phi where I is the current along the bundle. It cannot feed pulsar activity because of small \\Phi.

Footnote 2: This expression is invalid if z_{0}\\gtrsim r_{\\rm pc}, i.e. if 1-\\alpha\\lesssim\\Delta\\equiv 2^{3/2}(c/\\omega_{p}r_{\\rm pc})\\sim 6\\times 10^{- 5}\\,P\\,B_{13}^{-1/2}(m/m_{e})^{1/2}. A formal maximum p=\\Delta^{-1} (with \\alpha fine-tuned to 1) is an overestimate as it neglects the change in \\alpha(z).

A time dependence does not qualitatively change the character of the flow with 0<\\alpha<1: p(z,t) remains oscillatory because E_{\\parallel} always directs the flow toward v/c=\\alpha. Indeed, acceleration to v/c>\\alpha implies either \\rho/\\rho_{\\rm GJ}<1 or j/j_{B}>1. In both cases, E_{\\parallel} is generated to decelerate the flow: \\rho/\\rho_{\\rm GJ}<1 implies e\\partial E_{\\parallel}/\\partial z<0 (Gauss law), and j/j_{B}>1 implies e\\partial E_{\\parallel}/\\partial t<0 (self-induction). This negative feedback leads to the oscillatory behavior. E.g. perturbations with wavevectors {\\bf k}\\parallel{\\bf B} are simple Langmuir oscillations. Oscillations of amplitude E_{\\rm max} can nonlinearly interact with the plasma flow, heat it, and lose coherence. Turbulent pulsations of electric field can introduce anomalous resistivity and increase voltage. This, however, still does not lead to strong particle acceleration needed for pulsar activity.

The basic difference between the regimes \\alpha^{-1}<1 and \\alpha^{-1}>1 is due to the interplay of self-induction and electrostatic effects. Consider first the case \\alpha=0 (zero current). Clearly, neutrality \\rho=\\rho_{\\rm GJ} should be maintained; the needed charges will be pulled out from the star and a static atmosphere can form with \\rho\\approx\\rho_{\\rm GJ}. For a small \\alpha>0, the charges will slowly drift outward and will be replenished by new charges extracted from the polar cap by a small E_{\\parallel}. This picture can remain qualitatively the same with increasing \\alpha until \\alpha approaches 1. When \\alpha>1, the extracted charges with density \\rho=\\rho_{\\rm GJ} are unable to carry the required current j_{B} even if they move with v=c; then a large E_{\\parallel} will be induced. In essence, it is the vacuum charge density \\rho_{0}=-\\rho_{\\rm GJ} that helps avoid high voltages for 0<\\alpha<1: then the extracted plasma both neutralizes the tube and carries the imposed current. Such an accelerator is inefficient because E_{\\parallel} is dynamically screened on the small plasma scale z_{0}. This general argument should hold in full 3D time-dependent models of the open tube.

Hereafter, we focus on the regime \\alpha^{-1}<1 (\\alpha<0 or \\alpha>1), when strong particle acceleration is expected (Fig. 1). In absence of e^{\\pm} creation, the flow keeps accelerating until it approaches z\\sim r_{\\rm pc} where eE_{\\parallel} drops. Consider a simple case where \\alpha=const across the polar cap. If one imagines a global steady state with j=j_{B}, the electrostatic potential at z>r_{\\rm pc} would be (e.g. Ruderman & Sutherland 1975),

\\Phi=S_{\\perp}(\\rho-\\rho_{\\rm GJ})=\\left(1-\\frac{1}{\\alpha}\\right)S_{\\perp} \\frac{j_{B}}{c},  where S_{\\perp} is the cross section of the open tube. \\alpha^{-1}<1 implies e\\Phi>0, and the accelerated flow cannot overcome the potential barrier at the entrance to the thin-tube zone. It would have to decelerate and reverse at z\\sim r_{\\rm pc} (in an attempted steady state, the deceleration would increase density, making the potential barrier higher). Therefore, j cannot flow steadily. When j/j_{B} drops below unity, the induction current (4\\pi)^{-1}\\partial E_{\\parallel}/\\partial t=j_{B}-j is generated, and eE_{\\parallel} grows until j recovers back to j=j_{B}. Thus, in absence of e^{\\pm} creation, the charge-separated flow from the polar cap is intermittently reversed by the electrostatic barrier at z\\sim r_{\\rm pc} and then pushed through by the self-induction effect. The particle momentum p achieved in this unsteady regime is p_{\\rm max}\\sim(1-\\frac{1}{\\alpha})\\,\\xi^{2} (eq. 6).

## 4 Polar-cap accelerator with e^{\\pm} discharges

Accelerators with \\alpha<0 or \\alpha>1 are efficient and can easily ignite an e^{\\pm} discharge that screens E_{\\parallel} at a height h<r_{\\rm pc}. The voltage developed in the unscreened "gap" 0<z<h is

\\Phi\\sim 4\\pi(\\rho-\\rho_{\\rm GJ})\\,h^{2}=4\\pi\\left(1-{1\\over\\alpha}\\right){j_ {B}\\over c}\\,h^{2},\\qquad h<r_{\\rm pc}. 

The gap with \\alpha>1 was discussed by Sturrock (1971) and Kennel et al. (1979). It is different from the vacuum gap of Ruderman & Sutherland (1975), which formed because the star was unable to supply charges to keep \\rho=\\rho_{\\rm GJ}. The gap described by equation (8) forms because the imposed current j_{B} oversupplies charge density (\\rho/\\rho_{\\rm GJ}>1 when \\alpha>1) or supplies it with opposite sign (\\rho/\\rho_{\\rm GJ}<0 when \\alpha<0). The electric field in the gap is much stronger than in Arons & Scharlemann (1979) model. The maximal available potential drop \\Phi_{\\rm max}\\sim B\\Omega^{2}R^{3}/c^{2} is achieved if h\\sim r_{\\rm pc}. In observed pulsars h<r_{\\rm pc}.

The e^{\\pm} discharge develops as the accelerated primary particles (extracted from the polar cap) emit copious \\gamma-rays that convert to outgoing e^{\\pm}. The e^{\\pm} Lorentz factor \\gamma_{\\pm}\\sim 10^{2}-10^{3} is much smaller than the primary \\gamma_{1}\\sim eE_{\\parallel}z/mc^{2}. Therefore, the created -e charges (opposite in sign to j_{B}) are easily reversed by E_{\\parallel} and flow back to the polar cap. The reversal length in the unscreened E_{\\parallel} is l_{\\rm rev}\\sim\\left(\\gamma_{\\pm}/\\gamma_{1}\\right)z.

The backflow would be avoided only if the rate of e^{\\pm} production at z=h jumps from 0 to a high value, launching a steady dense e^{\\pm} outflow that screens E_{\\parallel} on a scale \\delta z_{\\rm scr}<l_{\\rm rev}. This, however, cannot happen for the following reason. The maximum charge density that may be created in \\delta z_{\\rm scr} by e^{\\pm} outflow without a backflow is3\\rho_{\\pm}\\sim-\\gamma_{\\pm}^{-2}{\\cal M}_{\\rm scr}j_{B}/c, where {\\cal M}_{\\rm scr} is multiplicity (number per primary particle) of e^{\\pm} created in \\delta z_{\\rm scr}. The screening of E_{\\parallel} requires -4\\pi\\delta z_{\\rm scr}\\rho_{\\pm}\\sim E_{\\parallel}, and \\delta z_{\\rm scr}<l_{\\rm rev} leads to condition {\\cal M}_{\\rm scr}>\\gamma_{\\pm}\\gamma_{1}^{2}\\,(r_{\\rm pc}/\\xi h)^{2}. It requires a huge {\\cal M}_{\\rm scr} and cannot be satisfied.4 One concludes that a strong backflow is inevitable in pair-producing accelerators with h<r_{\\rm pc}.

Footnote 3: To avoid the backflow, the e^{\\pm} outflow must be gently polarized, with a typical reduction of \\gamma_{-e} by a factor \\mathrel{\\hbox to 0.0pt{\\lower 3.0pt\\hbox{$\\mathchar 536$}\\hss}\\raise 2.0pt \\hbox{$\\mathchar 318$}}2; otherwise the dispersion \\Delta\\gamma_{-e}/\\gamma_{-e}={\\cal O}(1) would help form a strong backflow.

Footnote 4: Sharpest pair formation fronts occur in accelerators with \\gamma_{1}>10^{6}, where e^{\\pm} are created by curvature photons (e.g. Arons 1979). Other channels of pair production (significant at smaller \\gamma_{1}) create e^{\\pm} with a smooth distribution over z and help backflow formation.

A steady-state assumption for the discharge with a strong backflow leads to contradictions. First note that, in a steady state, the reversal of -e implies ed\\rho/dz>0. It steepens the growth of eE_{\\parallel}(z) at h\\mathrel{\\hbox to 0.0pt{\\lower 3.0pt\\hbox{$\\mathchar 536$}\\hss}\\raise 2.0pt \\hbox{$\\mathchar 318$}}z\\mathrel{\\hbox to 0.0pt{\\lower 3.0pt\\hbox{$\\mathchar 5 36$}\\hss}\\raise 2.0pt\\hbox{$\\mathchar 318$}}r_{\\rm pc} instead of screening E_{\\parallel}. Thus, all charges -e created at z<r_{\\rm pc} reverse in the unscreened E_{\\parallel}, creating a backflow of a large multiplicity {\\cal M}_{\\rm back}\\gg 1The backflowing charges reach the polar cap and contribute a fraction f to the total current, leaving the fraction (1-f) to be carried by charges extracted from the polar cap. In a steady state, the acceleration of extracted charges would be described by the same equations of SS 2 if one substitutes j=j_{B}(1-f) and replaces \\rho_{\\rm GJ} by \\rho_{\\rm GJ}+fj_{B}/c (the effective vacuum \\rho_{0} now includes the backflow charge density -fj_{B}/c). The new parameter \\alpha_{\\rm eff} is given by \\alpha_{\\rm eff}=j/(c\\rho_{\\rm GJ}+fj_{B})=\\alpha(1-f)/(1+\\alpha f). The presence of backflowing charges (f\\neq 0) changes the condition for gap formation from \\alpha^{-1}<1 to \\alpha_{\\rm eff}^{-1}<1, which is equivalent to

f<f_{\\star}\\equiv\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\left(1-\\frac{1}{\\alpha}\\right). 

The charge density in the relativistic accelerator is \\rho_{\\rm gap}\\approx fj_{B}/c-(1-f)j_{B}/c-\\rho_{\\rm GJ}=(1-2f-\\alpha^{-1})j_{B }/c. It must satisfy edE_{\\parallel}/dz=4\\pi e\\rho_{\\rm gap}>0, which explains the condition (9). One then finds: (1) In a steady accelerator with \\alpha>1, f cannot exceed \\frac{1}{2}. It permits a backflow multiplicity {\\cal M}_{\\rm back}\\equiv f/(1-f)<1. A steady e^{\\pm} discharge with \\alpha>1 is inconsistent because it would create {\\cal M}_{\\rm back}\\gg 1 that switches off the polar-cap accelerator. Similarly, a steady state is excluded for \\alpha<-1. (2) The accelerator with -1<\\alpha<0 could, in principle, have f\\to 1 and {\\cal M}_{\\rm back}\\gg 1. A steady state is, however, implausible for another reason: a significant rate of ingoing pair creation can be expected when {\\cal M}_{\\rm back}\\gg 1. It would lead to a runaway loop of e^{\\pm} creation in the accelerator, breaking the steady state. (3) A special case arises if f\\to f_{\\star}, which requires {\\cal M}_{\\rm back}={\\cal M}^{\\star}_{\\rm back}\\equiv(\\alpha-1)/(\\alpha+1). Note that it cannot happen if -1<\\alpha<1. For other \\alpha, {\\cal M}^{\\star}_{\\rm back}\\sim 1 (unless \\alpha is fine-tuned to -1). The accelerator should not permit a steady state with {\\cal M}_{\\rm back}={\\cal M}^{\\star}_{\\rm back}\\sim 1 because the discharge creates {\\cal M}_{\\rm back}\\gg 1 even if f\\to f_{\\star} (details will be given elsewhere).

We conclude that the e^{\\pm} discharge is unsteady, repeating on a timescale \\sim h/c. Following a discharge, f exceeds f_{\\star} and voltage is screened. Voltage is re-generated when e^{\\pm} leave the polar-cap region and f drops below f_{\\star}. In contrast to steady models, E_{\\parallel} is not described by an electrostatic potential \\Phi. Its time evolution obeys the Maxwell equation, \\partial E_{\\parallel}/\\partial t=4\\pi(j_{B}-j), which expresses the self-induction effect. The current will fluctuate around j_{B} (Levinson et al. 2005); in addition, the excitation of Alfven waves implies fluctuations of j_{B} itself (SS 5). A time-averaged {\\cal M}_{\\rm back}\\sim 1 can be expected in such discharges. Then the backflow energy flux is comparable to that of the primary particles. The resulting strong heating of the polar cap can conflict observations, posing a problem (cf. Arons 2008).

## 5 Alfven-wave pulsar

{\\bf E}\\neq 0 in the open tube implies that the tube rotates relative to the polar cap (e.g. Ruderman & Sutherland 1975). The rotational velocity of magnetic field lines is given by |{\\bf v}_{\\perp}|/c=|\\,{\\bf E}\\times\\,{\\bf B}|/B^{2}=E_{\\perp}/B where E\\ll B and E_{\\perp} is the component perpendicular to {\\bf B}. {\\bf v}_{\\perp} is the drift velocity of plasma; it may also be associated with rotation of magnetic energy since c\\,{\\bf E}\\times\\,{\\bf B}/4\\pi is the Poynting flux.

Consider now a time-dependent state, where the charge density and E_{\\perp} fluctuate on a vertical scale h and timescale t\\sim h/c. Such fluctuations will be accompanied by changes in v_{\\perp}, and the magnetic field lines will be twisted with amplitude

{\\delta B_{\\phi}\\over B}\\sim{\\delta v_{\\perp}t\\over h}\\sim{\\delta v_{\\perp} \\over c}\\sim{\\delta E_{\\perp}\\over B}. 

The corresponding fluctuation in magnetic energy density is \\delta(B^{2}/8\\pi)=\\delta\\,{\\bf B}\\cdot{\\bf B}/4\\pi+(\\delta\\,{\\bf B})^{2}/8\\pi \\sim(\\delta B_{\\phi})^{2}/4\\pi. The time-dependent twisting generates Alfven waves that propagate along the open tube with nearly speed of light and carry luminosity

L_{\\rm A}\\sim\\pi r_{\\rm pc}^{2}\\,c\\,{(\\delta B_{\\phi})^{2}\\over 4\\pi}\\sim{c \\over 4}\\,r_{\\rm pc}^{2}\\,(\\delta E_{\\perp})^{2}. 

For a gap of height h<r_{\\rm pc} with charge-density fluctuations \\delta\\rho, one can estimate \\delta E_{\\perp}\\sim 4\\pi\\delta\\rho\\,h. This gives

L_{\\rm A}\\sim{c(\\delta\\rho)^{2}\\over\\rho_{\\rm gap}j_{B}}\\,L_{\\rm gap},  where \\rho_{\\rm gap}=\\rho-\\rho_{\\rm GJ}\\sim\\Phi/4\\pi h^{2}, L_{\\rm gap}=I\\Phi is the time-averaged power dissipated in the gap, and I\\sim S_{\\perp}j_{B}. L_{\\rm A} should not exceed L_{\\rm gap}, since it is the gap accelerator that provides the energy for shaking the field lines. A related estimate of electro-magnetic dipole radiation from sparks is found in Fawley (1978).

The generated Alfven waves are ducted along the field lines and eventually suffer Landau damping (e.g. Lyubarsky 1996). However, part of L_{\\rm A} will convert to transverse electromagnetic waves in the emission region, where the modes do not adiabatically track. Jil, Lyubarsky, & Melikidze (2004) show that such conversion generally occurs for waves with frequency below the plasma frequency. This condition is satisfied for Alfven waves and a significant fraction of luminosity L_{\\rm A} is expected to escape as radio emission.

Usually considered mechanisms of pulsar radio emission are based on the two-stream instability in the e^{\\pm} outflow at large radii. The emerging radio spectrum is then determined by the plasma physics and possibly by curvature emission from plasma bunches created by the two-stream instability. By contrast, the emission fed by Alfven waves from the gap will have a spectrum controlled by the discharge behavior. One characteristic wavelength may be associated with the gap thickness h, roughly corresponding to frequency \\sim 10 MHz. However, fluctuations \\delta\\rho will occur also on scales \\lambda<h, producing waves of higher frequencies. Suchfluctuations can create \\delta E_{\\perp}\\sim 4\\pi\\,\\delta\\rho\\,\\lambda, and their contribution to Alfven luminosity is

L_{\\rm A}(\\lambda)\\sim 4\\pi^{2}c\\,r_{\\rm pc}^{2}\\lambda^{2}\\,(\\delta\\rho)^{2} \\sim L_{\\rm gap}\\,\\left(\\frac{c\\rho_{\\rm gap}}{j_{B}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\lambda }{h}\\right)^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\delta\\rho}{\\rho_{\\rm gap}}\\right)^{2}. 

A short scale available in the discharge is the screening scale l_{\\rm rev}\\ll h (SS 4). The electric field is screened by sudden formation of dense fronts of -e backflow on scale \\lambda\\sim l_{\\rm rev}, producing large fluctuations \\delta\\rho/\\rho_{\\rm GJ}\\gg 1, which can generate waves of frequency c/l_{\\rm rev}\\sim GHz. The spectrum of fluctuations in the e^{\\pm} discharge extends from \\sim c/h to c/l_{\\rm rev}. Numerical simulations of the discharge can help find the spectrum of escaping waves and their beaming.

